1
00:00:00,831 --> 00:00:07,271
My talk begins where my life nearly ended.

2
00:00:07,271 --> 00:00:12,042
In 2012, I stood in the red circle,

3
00:00:12,042 --> 00:00:14,878
and I gave a TED talk on social psychology.

4
00:00:14,878 --> 00:00:18,081
I was a junior professor at Harvard,

5
00:00:18,081 --> 00:00:22,386
and I spoke about feeling like an outsider.

6
00:00:22,386 --> 00:00:27,257
I spoke about how we lift our self-confidence

7
00:00:27,257 --> 00:00:32,663
and how our bodies affect the way we feel.

8
00:00:32,663 --> 00:00:36,800
The talk went viral online quickly

9
00:00:36,800 --> 00:00:43,073
and became one of the most viewed TED talks of all time.

10
00:00:43,073 --> 00:00:48,712
And that cracked my world open.

11
00:00:48,712 --> 00:00:51,548
In some beautiful ways.

12
00:00:51,548 --> 00:00:58,155
(Applause)

13
00:00:58,155 --> 00:01:04,061
I saw the most luminous parts of people.

14
00:01:04,061 --> 00:01:08,465
And then I saw the darkest parts.

15
00:01:08,465 --> 00:01:12,569
When I became the target

16
00:01:12,569 --> 00:01:16,873
of a sustained bullying campaign.

17
00:01:16,873 --> 00:01:20,277
Not by trolls on the internet --

18
00:01:20,277 --> 00:01:23,480
I learned to dismiss them quickly --

19
00:01:23,480 --> 00:01:25,782
but by colleagues,

20
00:01:25,782 --> 00:01:30,053
by tenured professors, department chairs

21
00:01:30,053 --> 00:01:32,990
and journal editors

22
00:01:32,990 --> 00:01:35,993
who used their names -- their real names --

23
00:01:35,993 --> 00:01:39,896
to attack me relentlessly,

24
00:01:39,896 --> 00:01:42,232
for many years.

25
00:01:42,232 --> 00:01:44,568
They publicly declared

26
00:01:44,568 --> 00:01:49,673
that I had no serious conception of the term science.

27
00:01:49,673 --> 00:01:54,211
That what I'd proposed was stupid and unintuitive.

28
00:01:54,211 --> 00:01:56,013
That I'd abandoned academia

29
00:01:56,013 --> 00:01:59,149
for a lucrative career selling pseudoscience

30
00:01:59,149 --> 00:02:02,919
to a mostly women audience.

31
00:02:02,919 --> 00:02:07,658
They created a narrative about our work on power posing

32
00:02:07,658 --> 00:02:10,093
that it had been debunked.

33
00:02:10,093 --> 00:02:13,397
But here's the thing.

34
00:02:13,397 --> 00:02:16,967
Our work on power posing hadn't been debunked.

35
00:02:16,967 --> 00:02:18,402
In fact,

36
00:02:18,402 --> 00:02:22,472
the central finding, the most important finding,

37
00:02:22,472 --> 00:02:24,374
was that our posture,

38
00:02:24,374 --> 00:02:27,010
how expansive or contractive it is,

39
00:02:27,010 --> 00:02:28,945
affects the way we feel,

40
00:02:28,945 --> 00:02:32,282
how powerful [and] confident we feel.

41
00:02:32,282 --> 00:02:34,885
It was replicated dozens of times

42
00:02:34,885 --> 00:02:38,422
in independent studies by labs all over the world,

43
00:02:38,422 --> 00:02:41,825
using better methods than those that we used,

44
00:02:41,825 --> 00:02:44,094
and even by our skeptics.

45
00:02:44,094 --> 00:02:46,663
In multiple meta-analyses,

46
00:02:46,663 --> 00:02:52,069
including one with 88 studies in nearly 10,000 participants,

47
00:02:52,069 --> 00:02:55,372
they landed on the same conclusion:

48
00:02:55,372 --> 00:02:58,308
Expansive versus contractive posture

49
00:02:58,308 --> 00:03:03,714
affects how powerful or powerless we feel.

50
00:03:03,714 --> 00:03:08,285
The behaviors and the hormones were more complicated,

51
00:03:08,285 --> 00:03:11,755
and there were non-replications.

52
00:03:11,755 --> 00:03:14,958
In the science that I was trained in

53
00:03:14,958 --> 00:03:18,829
by my dear advisor,

54
00:03:18,829 --> 00:03:24,501
that would have led to constructive and friendly inquiries,

55
00:03:24,501 --> 00:03:29,306
collaborations, to figure out what was different about these studies.

56
00:03:29,306 --> 00:03:30,507
Were there moderators

57
00:03:30,507 --> 00:03:34,945
that affected why you got this effect and we got this effect?

58
00:03:34,945 --> 00:03:38,115
Science should interrogate itself.

59
00:03:38,115 --> 00:03:42,085
That is how knowledge advances --

60
00:03:42,085 --> 00:03:43,887
that should happen.

61
00:03:43,887 --> 00:03:46,656
But that's not what happened in my case.

62
00:03:46,656 --> 00:03:49,760
Science didn't interrogate itself.

63
00:03:49,760 --> 00:03:53,096
It stopped itself.

64
00:03:53,096 --> 00:03:57,701
And it silenced a scientist, a junior scientist,

65
00:03:57,701 --> 00:04:00,904
who also had a lot of other research

66
00:04:00,904 --> 00:04:05,375
on stereotyping and prejudice and intergroup relations.

67
00:04:05,375 --> 00:04:08,078
Two tenured Ivy League professors

68
00:04:08,078 --> 00:04:10,747
told a "New York Times" reporter

69
00:04:10,747 --> 00:04:13,583
that they would have defended me publicly

70
00:04:13,583 --> 00:04:19,756
had they not been afraid of becoming targets themselves.

71
00:04:19,756 --> 00:04:21,625
Friends wrote privately,

72
00:04:21,625 --> 00:04:23,727
"I'm so sorry this is happening to you.

73
00:04:23,727 --> 00:04:26,029
These guys are horrible."

74
00:04:26,029 --> 00:04:30,233
But wouldn't look at me publicly.

75
00:04:30,233 --> 00:04:32,002
I couldn't eat,

76
00:04:32,002 --> 00:04:33,837
I didn't sleep,

77
00:04:33,837 --> 00:04:36,773
I worked all the time defending myself

78
00:04:36,773 --> 00:04:39,543
from constant incoming attacks,

79
00:04:39,543 --> 00:04:43,180
non-stop, relentless.

80
00:04:43,180 --> 00:04:47,818
It was very hard to be present for my son and my husband.

81
00:04:47,818 --> 00:04:53,356
I'm sorry.

82
00:04:53,356 --> 00:04:59,296
And I chose to walk away from my job at Harvard

83
00:04:59,296 --> 00:05:01,698
a year before going up for tenure.

84
00:05:01,698 --> 00:05:05,135
Not because I was forced to,

85
00:05:05,135 --> 00:05:10,740
but because I knew that staying meant certain death.

86
00:05:10,740 --> 00:05:13,710
And so ...

87
00:05:13,710 --> 00:05:16,179
Eventually,

88
00:05:16,179 --> 00:05:20,917
I tried to take my own life twice,

89
00:05:20,917 --> 00:05:25,655
because this kind of bullying

90
00:05:25,655 --> 00:05:32,495
is simultaneously this horrible public spectacle

91
00:05:32,495 --> 00:05:35,732
of shame where you're in a spotlight.

92
00:05:35,732 --> 00:05:38,101
And at the same time,

93
00:05:38,101 --> 00:05:42,772
it's a total erasure of who you are

94
00:05:42,772 --> 00:05:46,843
and what you've done.

95
00:05:46,843 --> 00:05:50,547
Many targets don't survive.

96
00:05:50,547 --> 00:05:56,653
And I do want to say, before I go forward, to the targets:

97
00:05:56,653 --> 00:05:58,722
I understand.

98
00:05:58,722 --> 00:06:04,361
I see you, and I know that what seems incredible --

99
00:06:04,361 --> 00:06:06,930
happened.

100
00:06:06,930 --> 00:06:10,433
But.

101
00:06:10,433 --> 00:06:14,504
This is not a talk about despair.

102
00:06:14,504 --> 00:06:19,142
This is a talk about earned hope.

103
00:06:19,142 --> 00:06:21,945
I've been a social psychologist for 25 years,

104
00:06:21,945 --> 00:06:27,050
studying the forces that affect how we treat each other and judge each other.

105
00:06:27,050 --> 00:06:29,552
But I've spent the last eight

106
00:06:29,552 --> 00:06:37,527
focused on how cruelty becomes collective.

107
00:06:37,527 --> 00:06:40,964
How truth becomes distorted.

108
00:06:40,964 --> 00:06:47,938
How people decide that one person should be pushed out of the group.

109
00:06:47,938 --> 00:06:50,974
But also, more importantly,

110
00:06:50,974 --> 00:06:52,842
what are the things that we can do

111
00:06:52,842 --> 00:06:59,282
to prevent those cultures from taking hold before they harden.

112
00:06:59,282 --> 00:07:00,750
And what I've learned is this.

113
00:07:00,750 --> 00:07:05,722
First, bullying is not conflict.

114
00:07:05,722 --> 00:07:07,724
It's not a debate,

115
00:07:07,724 --> 00:07:09,659
it's not criticism,

116
00:07:09,659 --> 00:07:13,463
it's not humor that you're too sensitive to take well.

117
00:07:13,463 --> 00:07:16,900
It is a deliberately targeted,

118
00:07:16,900 --> 00:07:21,304
serial, escalating pattern of attacks

119
00:07:21,304 --> 00:07:24,374
carried out by multiple people,

120
00:07:24,374 --> 00:07:28,244
and it's intended to destroy a person's reputation

121
00:07:28,244 --> 00:07:31,181
and their ability to contribute.

122
00:07:31,181 --> 00:07:36,286
It unfolds in predictable stages.

123
00:07:36,286 --> 00:07:41,558
And it usually ends with someone being completely silenced

124
00:07:41,558 --> 00:07:44,661
or disappearing completely.

125
00:07:44,661 --> 00:07:46,596
This is really important.

126
00:07:46,596 --> 00:07:48,698
Bullies are rare.

127
00:07:48,698 --> 00:07:49,899
They're rare.

128
00:07:49,899 --> 00:07:52,168
Most of us are not bullies.

129
00:07:52,168 --> 00:07:55,138
Bullies share one thing:

130
00:07:55,138 --> 00:07:57,107
a worldview.

131
00:07:57,107 --> 00:07:58,908
In their worldview,

132
00:07:58,908 --> 00:08:00,910
the world is hostile,

133
00:08:00,910 --> 00:08:04,147
people are fundamentally bad,

134
00:08:04,147 --> 00:08:06,916
everything of value is scarce,

135
00:08:06,916 --> 00:08:10,653
it’s all zero-sum.

136
00:08:10,653 --> 00:08:13,423
Your achievement is their loss.

137
00:08:13,423 --> 00:08:19,462
They have an insatiable need for recognition and status and dominance,

138
00:08:19,462 --> 00:08:22,532
and they prove their dominance

139
00:08:22,532 --> 00:08:27,771
by publicly demonstrating that they can force a target,

140
00:08:27,771 --> 00:08:31,474
a valuable target, to fully submit.

141
00:08:31,474 --> 00:08:35,578
That's what bullying is.

142
00:08:35,578 --> 00:08:42,786
Bullying has been on the rise among adults for the last 15 years.

143
00:08:42,786 --> 00:08:49,692
Digital platforms make bullying faster, easier, louder.

144
00:08:49,692 --> 00:08:55,031
And the bullies' lies last forever now.

145
00:08:55,031 --> 00:08:57,467
So ...

146
00:08:57,467 --> 00:09:02,906
What do we do?

147
00:09:02,906 --> 00:09:09,946
The first thing to understand is that bullies need us.

148
00:09:09,946 --> 00:09:12,549
Bullies need an audience.

149
00:09:12,549 --> 00:09:16,553
They need people to carry out their agenda.

150
00:09:16,553 --> 00:09:20,256
When we just watch and remain silent,

151
00:09:20,256 --> 00:09:23,159
we're what I call passive bystanders.

152
00:09:23,159 --> 00:09:27,497
When we join in and amplify and share an attack,

153
00:09:27,497 --> 00:09:31,167
we're what I call accessory bullies.

154
00:09:31,167 --> 00:09:34,504
But if we refuse our participation,

155
00:09:34,504 --> 00:09:37,841
a lone bully is impotent.

156
00:09:37,841 --> 00:09:41,678
Without us, a bully is impotent.

157
00:09:41,678 --> 00:09:46,449
(Applause)

158
00:09:46,449 --> 00:09:51,087
Second, we need to learn to spot the opening.

159
00:09:51,087 --> 00:09:53,022
When we get in early,

160
00:09:53,022 --> 00:09:55,558
we can totally derail this.

161
00:09:55,558 --> 00:09:58,561
It starts with what I call a “bully test.”

162
00:09:58,561 --> 00:10:02,765
The bully tests the audience to see if they can get away

163
00:10:02,765 --> 00:10:04,467
with bullying that person.

164
00:10:04,467 --> 00:10:09,205
It might be a really inappropriate comment in a professional setting,

165
00:10:09,205 --> 00:10:11,608
or it might be an accusation

166
00:10:11,608 --> 00:10:14,110
against someone that’s reputation-destroying,

167
00:10:14,110 --> 00:10:16,446
but not backed by evidence.

168
00:10:16,446 --> 00:10:20,950
When we hear those things and we stay silent or we laugh,

169
00:10:20,950 --> 00:10:24,254
we're just saying to the bully, "Go forward."

170
00:10:24,254 --> 00:10:27,157
And that's when they launch their attack.

171
00:10:27,157 --> 00:10:29,893
We also need to practice social bravery.

172
00:10:29,893 --> 00:10:33,363
These small risks that we take to defend each other,

173
00:10:33,363 --> 00:10:35,532
to support each other interpersonally.

174
00:10:35,532 --> 00:10:37,901
And social bravery is risky.

175
00:10:37,901 --> 00:10:40,703
So we need to distribute the risk across people.

176
00:10:40,703 --> 00:10:42,005
We need to do it together.

177
00:10:42,005 --> 00:10:45,341
It is so much easier for two people to do it

178
00:10:45,341 --> 00:10:47,377
than for one person to do it.

179
00:10:47,377 --> 00:10:51,514
When one bystander steps in, in a way that’s noticeable,

180
00:10:51,514 --> 00:10:53,583
others step in.

181
00:10:53,583 --> 00:10:57,954
And we know from research on changing social conventions

182
00:10:57,954 --> 00:11:01,558
that when about 25 percent of people step in,

183
00:11:01,558 --> 00:11:04,561
they can flip a social norm completely.

184
00:11:04,561 --> 00:11:06,829
Bullying is not a personal problem.

185
00:11:06,829 --> 00:11:08,998
It's a collective problem.

186
00:11:08,998 --> 00:11:11,034
And we can fix it.

187
00:11:11,034 --> 00:11:14,437
So after eight years of writing this book,

188
00:11:14,437 --> 00:11:17,574
I turned it in a few months ago.

189
00:11:17,574 --> 00:11:19,175
Right after that,

190
00:11:19,175 --> 00:11:21,611
I traveled to Greeley, Colorado,

191
00:11:21,611 --> 00:11:25,148
a small city in the High Plains

192
00:11:25,148 --> 00:11:26,616
that was raw,

193
00:11:26,616 --> 00:11:29,152
politically divided, on edge.

194
00:11:29,152 --> 00:11:33,456
I could feel the vigilance in the air when I looked at them.

195
00:11:33,456 --> 00:11:36,859
I spoke about personal power and trust,

196
00:11:36,859 --> 00:11:41,864
and at the end the moderator said her last question to me was this.

197
00:11:41,864 --> 00:11:43,032
She said,

198
00:11:43,032 --> 00:11:46,135
"Is there one piece of leadership advice

199
00:11:46,135 --> 00:11:48,972
that you haven't really shared?"

200
00:11:48,972 --> 00:11:51,307
And I said, “Yeah.

201
00:11:51,307 --> 00:11:56,412
We'll never win by bullying the bullies.

202
00:11:56,412 --> 00:12:00,083
We cannot validate their worldview.

203
00:12:00,083 --> 00:12:04,387
As soon as we tell them that the world has to be brutal,

204
00:12:04,387 --> 00:12:05,888
they say, ’See?

205
00:12:05,888 --> 00:12:10,093
That’s why I have to behave this way.“’

206
00:12:10,093 --> 00:12:14,163
I told them you don’t have to show how much you can hate

207
00:12:14,163 --> 00:12:19,669
to prove how much you care, or to prove that you belong.

208
00:12:19,669 --> 00:12:24,173
And I looked out and I saw their shoulders relax.

209
00:12:24,173 --> 00:12:27,844
I saw a flicker of hope in their faces.

210
00:12:27,844 --> 00:12:32,715
I felt them collectively exhale.

211
00:12:32,715 --> 00:12:36,119
And the next day a woman wrote to me.

212
00:12:36,119 --> 00:12:39,455
And she said, "I was at your talk.

213
00:12:39,455 --> 00:12:43,526
And I noticed something interesting as I left the venue.

214
00:12:43,526 --> 00:12:47,830
The crowd was unusually cheerful and courteous.

215
00:12:47,830 --> 00:12:52,101
Several drive aisles wove together toward the exits,

216
00:12:52,101 --> 00:12:55,672
and everyone was alternating and letting each other in.

217
00:12:55,672 --> 00:12:57,807
No one was saying with their actions

218
00:12:57,807 --> 00:13:01,577
that they were more important than another person.

219
00:13:01,577 --> 00:13:04,280
I just wanted to share the observation

220
00:13:04,280 --> 00:13:08,484
that you made an immediate, positive and tangible difference."

221
00:13:08,484 --> 00:13:15,792
(Applause)

222
00:13:15,792 --> 00:13:18,061
We want to be neighbors again.

223
00:13:18,061 --> 00:13:20,530
We want to go to our kids' friends' birthday parties

224
00:13:20,530 --> 00:13:23,933
and not worry about an explosion.

225
00:13:23,933 --> 00:13:27,170
We don't want to have to show how much we hate

226
00:13:27,170 --> 00:13:31,240
to prove how much we care.

227
00:13:31,240 --> 00:13:34,877
Bullies' power is borrowed power.

228
00:13:34,877 --> 00:13:39,782
And we can refuse to give it to them.

229
00:13:39,782 --> 00:13:45,321
We all have access to personal power, and with that personal power,

230
00:13:45,321 --> 00:13:48,458
we can commit small acts of social bravery,

231
00:13:48,458 --> 00:13:50,693
supporting each other,

232
00:13:50,693 --> 00:13:53,329
challenging bullies.

233
00:13:53,329 --> 00:13:56,432
So I invite you please, to join me.

234
00:13:56,432 --> 00:13:58,935
Let's find that collective power

235
00:13:58,935 --> 00:14:02,372
and turn bullying around.

236
00:14:02,372 --> 00:14:05,408
And one more thing.

237
00:14:05,408 --> 00:14:10,380
(Cheers and applause)

238
00:14:10,380 --> 00:14:13,049
Thank you.

239
00:14:13,049 --> 00:14:14,317
Thank you so much.

240
00:14:14,317 --> 00:14:18,317
(Applause)
